<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panagyurishte Treasure</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Panagyurishte Treasure is a Thracian hoard of nine gold vessels found in 1949 near Panagyurishte in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Weighing over 6 kilograms, it includes a phiale, an amphora-rhyton and several animal-headed rhyta decorated with scenes from Greek myth, and dates from the late 4th or early 3rd century BCE. It is one of the finest surviving examples of Thracian elite metalwork.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panagyurishte-treasure.jpg" alt="Panagyurishte Treasure" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ann Wuyts · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sofia_-_Panagyurishte_Thracian_Gold_Treasure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hoards such as the Rogozen treasure, more than 160 silver vessels, several inscribed with the names of Odrysian kings, and the gold <b>Panagyurishte</b> treasure of the late fourth or early third century BCE show the scale of elite display.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
